{title: Abide with Me}
{lyricist: Henry F. Lyte}
{composer: William H. Monk}
{year: 1847}
{meta: tune-year 1861}
{key: C}
{time: 4/4}
{meta: tune EVENTIDE}
{meta: meter 10.10.10.10}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to EVENTIDE}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags comfort, evening, funeral}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
A[C]bide with [G]me; [C]fast falls the [F]even[C]tide;
The [C]darkness [Am]deepens; [D7]Lord, with [G]me a[G7]bide;
When [C]other [G]helpers [Am]fail and [Em]comforts [F]flee,
Help [C]of the [F]helpless, [C]O a[G7]bide with [C]me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Swift [C]to its [G]close [C]ebbs out life's [F]little [C]day;
Earth's [C]joys grow [Am]dim; its [D7]glories [G]pass a[G7]way;
Change [C]and de[G]cay in [Am]all a[Em]round I [F]see;
O [C]Thou who [F]changest [C]not, a[G7]bide with [C]me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
I [C]need Thy [G]presence [C]every [F]passing [C]hour;
What [C]but Thy [Am]grace can [D7]foil the [G]tempter's [G7]power?
Who, [C]like Thy[G]self, my [Am]guide and [Em]stay can [F]be?
Through [C]cloud and [F]sunshine, [C]Lord, a[G7]bide with [C]me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Hold [C]Thou Thy [G]cross be[C]fore my [F]closing [C]eyes;
Shine [C]through the [Am]gloom and [D7]point me [G]to the [G7]skies;
Heaven's [C]morning [G]breaks, and [Am]earth's vain [Em]shadows [F]flee;
In [C]life, in [F]death, O [C]Lord, a[G7]bide with [C]me.
{end_of_verse}
